𝐌𝐨𝐬𝐭 𝐛𝐚𝐜𝐤𝐞𝐧𝐝 𝐩𝐫𝐨𝐣𝐞𝐜𝐭𝐬 𝐝𝐨𝐧’𝐭 𝐛𝐫𝐞𝐚𝐤 𝐮𝐧𝐝𝐞𝐫 𝐥𝐨𝐚𝐝 — 𝐭𝐡𝐞𝐲 𝐛𝐫𝐞𝐚𝐤 𝐮𝐧𝘥𝐞𝐫 𝐚𝐦𝐛𝐢𝐠𝐮𝐢𝐭𝐲.

Not 𝘱𝘦𝘳𝘧𝘰𝘳𝘮𝘢𝘯𝘤𝘦 issues.
Not 𝘴𝘤𝘢𝘭𝘪𝘯𝘨.
But 𝐮𝐧𝐝𝐞𝐟𝐢𝐧𝐞𝐝 𝐛𝐞𝐡𝐚𝐯𝐢𝐨𝐫 when things go 𝘴𝘭𝘪𝘨𝘩𝘵𝘭𝘺 wrong.

What happens when:
•  Input is technically valid but semantically wrong?
•  A resource exists but violates an invariant?
•  A request fails halfway through the lifecycle?

Early on, I noticed these cases leaking through controllers, services, and views in inconsistent ways. That’s fine for demos but it’s poison for systems you want to reason about.

So I paused feature work and fixed the failure model.

In this case, the project happens to be a URL shortener, Simple at first sight but a hundred ways it can break.

What changed:
1. Explicit domain-level exceptions instead of generic runtime errors
2. A central exception-handling filter so failures have a single, predictable exit path
3. Validation and code generation moved into dedicated utilities instead of ad-hoc logic
4. Custom error pages with a shared layout and minimal CSS — errors are part of the product, not an afterthought

The result isn’t more features.
It’s a system where I can explain why a request failed by reading the code.


That’s the bar I want before adding users, auth, or scale.
